<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Taylor Coleridge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1834">1834</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Taylor Coleridge was an English poet, literary critic, philosopher and theologian, born at Ottery St Mary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, who was a founder of the Romantic Movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and one of the Lake Poets with his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">William Wordsworth</a>. He wrote The Rime of the Ancient Mariner and Kubla Khan, and the prose work Biographia Literaria. His criticism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a> was influential, he helped introduce German idealist philosophy to England, and he coined the phrase suspension of disbelief. Treated with laudanum for anxiety, depression and ill health, he had a lifelong opium addiction. He influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-waldo-emerson">Ralph Waldo Emerson</a> and American transcendentalism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-taylor-coleridge.jpg" alt="Samuel Taylor Coleridge" width="330" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coleridge.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Coleridge was the youngest of ten children of the Reverend John Coleridge, vicar of St Mary's Church in Ottery St Mary and headmaster of the King's School, by his second wife, Anne Bowden. After his father died in 1781, the eight-year-old was sent to Christ's Hospital, a charity school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he studied and wrote poetry and became friends with Charles Lamb. At Jesus College he met radical political and theological ideas and collaborated with Robert Southey on the play The Fall of Robespierre. The two planned a commune called Pantisocracy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, which was abandoned. In 1795 he married Sara Fricker, but the marriage was unhappy and they were separated by 1804.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1798 he accepted an annuity of £150 a year from Josiah Wedgwood II on condition that he give up the ministry. His Conversation poems, among them The Eolian Harp and Frost at Midnight, and The Rime of the Ancient Mariner gave the language the albatross around one's neck. Kubla Khan and Christabel were never finished. In 1804 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, where he served as Acting Public Secretary under Alexander Ball, returning in 1806. His opium addiction, at times as much as two quarts of laudanum a week, took over his life: he separated from his wife in 1808, quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">William Wordsworth</a> in 1810 and lost part of his annuity in 1811.</p><p>His lectures on Shakespeare and Milton in the winter of 1810–11 founded much of his reputation as a critic. They were irregular, since he often prepared only loose notes, but his lecture on Hamlet of 2 January 1812 influenced studies of the play from then on. Biographia Literaria appeared in 1817; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-s-eliot">T. S. Eliot</a> called him perhaps the foremost of English critics. Early a political radical and enthusiast for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, he later took a more conservative view, in the manner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Coleridge lived in his last years on The Grove in Highgate with a doctor he hoped might cure him, and he died on 25 July 1834. After the vault at the Old Highgate Chapel became derelict, an international fundraising appeal in 1961 paid for the coffins to be moved to St Michael's Church, Highgate. His Aids to Reflection (1825) and its interpretation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a> influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-waldo-emerson">Ralph Waldo Emerson</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Taylor Coleridge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…his preface to the second edition of Lyrical Ballads in 1800 Wordsworth declared that “all good poetry is the spontaneous overflow of powerful feelings” and sought poetry in ordinary life and in the language people really speak, while <b>Samuel Taylor Coleridge</b> gave the supernatural a new imaginative seriousness.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
